# ---------------------------------------------------------------- follow-ups, sources, wrong, not done
follow = f"""
<h2 id="follow">Follow-ups: drivers, not literal answers</h2>
<ol>
<li><b>F1 · The point-in-time universe, once, on Fly.</b> Sections 1d, 3c and 4 all bend under today's-cap typing (mega caps look strong because they became mega caps; small caps look doomed because they shrank; the laggards' rebound is measured on survivors). One Fly one-off over the R2 day files (the scout design's nightly file) gives monthly membership and cap from 2003. Re-run 1d, 3, 4 and 5 on it. Cost: one job, one evening; every later study inherits it.</li>
<li><b>F2 · The Geiger percentile readout.</b> Not a change to the bar: a second small number, "the Nth percentile of its own history", computed nightly for every served name from the replay in this lane (the maths are the Hub's, 0.15 s per name). It answers "is this unusual for this name" without touching the Equalizer. Proposal for the Geiger tab and the Station chip.</li>
<li><b>F3 · Mean reversion in the beaten names, as a study of its own.</b> The strongest pooled edge on this page is the opposite of the pullback cell: weak trend + weak momentum, the low composite rungs, the worst-decile fallers after a low. It needs the cost line, the point-in-time universe and the section-3 flags as its stop before it becomes a card. Design: the scout tier screens it; the Hub shows only the base rate.</li>
<li><b>F4 · Confluence as a two-gauge readout, not a score.</b> The record says RSI percentile triggers and put/call confirms, the VIX level only sizes the decline. A "fear pair" line on the regime strip proposal (statistician-2, section 8): RSI percentile and put/call 5-day percentile side by side, with the 21-session base rate for the pair printed. No composite, because the proxy added nothing over its best part.</li>
<li><b>F5 · The sigma-day cluster on the USUAL DAY strip.</b> The one steady thing about a day like today is that more such days follow. Print it: "days like this were followed by another top-decile down day in {pc(tclass['more_ahead'])} of the next ten sessions (any day {pc(tclass['more_ahead_base'])})". Data is already in the table; it is a one-line change with a test.</li>
<li><b>F6 · Persistence of winners, measured properly.</b> Section 3d found prior-year winners repeat at twice the base rate. That is the momentum factor, and it deserves the alphalens treatment the method page proposed: monthly ranking by 12-month return, quantile forward returns, turnover, on the F1 universe. It is the "bet on winners" half of Alan's sentence, with a number.</li>
<li><b>F7 · The lag of the leaders, measured from the low itself vs from confirmation.</b> Statistician-2 found −1.5 points from the low; this page −0.4 from confirmation, ten sessions later. The difference is the first ten sessions, which nobody can trade. A short note that reconciles the two numbers and fixes which one goes on the stats tab.</li>
<li><b>F8 · Gold's break, tracked.</b> The 2025–26 run is the third-widest on the list and its fall so far sits in the middle of the list. A small tracker on the commodities room: sessions since the peak, fall so far, and the two comparable cases (2006, 2011) drawn beside it. Research, not a call.</li>
<li><b>F9 · Intraday rungs.</b> The Geiger replay here covers the daily rungs only (about two-fifths of the Equalizer's weight). The intraday history needed to replay the 3h–12h rungs exists on the chart API for a few months; a stored history of published readings (the geiger-review lane's stored-history speed test) is the way to get the full composite as a statistic. Ask: keep every nightly /geiger publish.</li>
<li><b>F10 · Steal what works.</b> Read on GitHub for this lane: the public fear &amp; greed reconstructions (seven parts, own percentiles) were reused here; the block bootstrap and false-discovery tools from <code>arch</code> and <code>statsmodels</code> were reimplemented in 200 lines of numpy so the studies run on this Mac with no install. The next thing to borrow is <code>alphalens-reloaded</code> for F6, and the RRG reimplementation the sector lane already did for the wings.</li>
</ol>
"""
